21st in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits are an under center rate that was among the highest and the 3rd lowest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Seahawks offense has been a little below average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are an under center rate that was among the highest and a shotgun rate that was among the lowest in the league. Sam Darnold has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Jaxon Smith-Njigba, who has drawn 36% of the targets.","defense":"The Seahawks have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 2nd or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been EPA allowed per rush, where it leads the league, and it has no real weak spot. Leonard Williams has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Ernest Jones, who has 3 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they have the 3rd lowest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Seahawks have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 93% of field goals, which is well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 23% of the money on the books.","players":"Sam Darnold has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,290 yards. Jaxon Smith-Njigba has been the clear top target with 36% of the looks and 867 receiving yards. Emanuel Wilson has handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Ernest Jones leads the team with 3 sacks and Julian Love has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Brian Fleury is calling the offense and Mike Macdonald is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Titans and the 2022 Panthers. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Bears and the 2022 Titans.","trends":"The offense went from 13th in EPA per play in 2025 to 21st so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 2nd in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 2nd so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was screen rate, which went from 6% to 12%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}